************** G E N E R A L   I N F O R M A T I O N *************3025 1451   22
                                                                  3025 1451   23
This evaluated data file is based on a software system built      3025 1451   24
around the nuclear model code TALYS [kon23], version 2.0. It is   3025 1451   25
part of a large collection of isotopic evaluations, all created by3025 1451   26
running the system with either adjusted or default input          3025 1451   27
parameters. This means that the data in the entire collection have3025 1451   28
been tested in detail for many isotopes against individual        3025 1451   29
experimental data, while for other isotopes it is only as good as 3025 1451   30
the global quality of TALYS at the moment. The mutual quality of  3025 1451   31
all individual data files is however consistent: The same set of  3025 1451   32
nuclear models is used and, equally important, the same ENDF-6    3025 1451   33
formatting procedures for each isotope. The data file has been    3025 1451   34
created automatically using the ENDF-6 format generator TEFAL.    3025 1451   35
                                                                  3025 1451   36
The resulting data file provides a complete representation of     3025 1451   37
nuclear data needed for transport, activation, damage, heating,   3025 1451   38
radioactivity, and shielding applications over the incident       3025 1451   39
photon energy range from 1. to 200. MeV.                          3025 1451   40
                                                                  3025 1451   41
All transport and activation data for neutrons, charged particles,3025 1451   42
photons and residual nuclides are filed. This includes cross      3025 1451   43
sections, angular distributions, double-differential spectra,     3025 1451   44
isomeric production, discrete and continuum photon production     3025 1451   45
cross sections, residual production cross sections and recoils.   3025 1451   46
This general purpose evaluation can thus be used as both          3025 1451   47
transport and activation library.                                 3025 1451   48
                                                                  3025 1451   49
A complete set of covariance data is included, which is stored    3025 1451   50
in MF31-40.                                                       3025 1451   51
                                                                  3025 1451   52
**** N U C L I D E    S P E C I F I C   I N F O R M A T I O N ****3025 1451   53
                                                                  3025 1451   54
               Adjusted TALYS input parameters                    3025 1451   55
                                                                  3025 1451   56
sgradjust 34 76 1.08 e1                                           3025 1451   57
                                                                  3025 1451   58
***************************** T A L Y S **************************3025 1451   59
                                                                  3025 1451   60
TALYS is a computer code system for the prediction and analysis   3025 1451   61
of nuclear reactions. TALYS simulates reactions that involve      3025 1451   62
neutrons, gamma-rays, protons, deuterons, tritons, helions and    3025 1451   63
alpha-particles, in the 1 keV - 200 MeV energy range. This is     3025 1451   64
achieved by implementing a suite of nuclear reaction models into  3025 1451   65
a single code system. This nuclear data file is based on a        3025 1451   66
theoretical analysis that utilizes the optical model, compound    3025 1451   67
nucleus statistical theory, fission, direct reactions and         3025 1451   68
pre-equilibrium processes, in combination with databases and      3025 1451   69
models for nuclear structure. The following output of TALYS is    3025 1451   70
stored in this data file:                                         3025 1451   71
                                                                  3025 1451   72
- Total, elastic and non-elastic cross sections                   3025 1451   73
- Inelastic cross sections to discrete states                     3025 1451   74
- Exclusive channel cross sections, e.g. (n,g), (n,2n), (n,np),.. 3025 1451   75
- Inelastic scattering angular distributions to discrete states   3025 1451   76
- Exclusive channel energy spectra                                3025 1451   77
- Exclusive channel double-differential spectra                   3025 1451   78
- Exclusive gamma production for discrete states and continuum    3025 1451   79
- Elastic scattering angular distributions                        3025 1451   80
- Recoil data                                                     3025 1451   81
- Total particle cross sections, e.g. (g,xn), (g,xp),..           3025 1451   82
- Total particle energy spectra                                   3025 1451   83
- Total particle double-differential spectra                      3025 1451   84
- Isomeric and ground state cross sections                        3025 1451   85
- Residual production cross sections                              3025 1451   86
                                                                  3025 1451   87
For a description of the used nuclear models and their            3025 1451   88
parameterization we refer to [kon19]. If for this specific        3025 1451   89
nuclide we deviate from the global parameterization, it is made   3025 1451   90
explicit above under Nuclide Specific Information.                3025 1451   91
                                                                  3025 1451   92
***************** F I L E   I N F O R M A T I O N ****************3025 1451   93
                                                                  3025 1451   94
##### MF1: GENERAL INFORMATION                                    3025 1451   95
                                                                  3025 1451   96
- MT451    : Descriptive data and directory                       3025 1451   97
                                                                  3025 1451   98
This text and the full directory of used MF/MT sections.          3025 1451   99
                                                                  3025 1451  100
##### MF3: REACTION CROSS SECTIONS                                3025 1451  101
                                                                  3025 1451  102
All the data present in the following MT-sections have been       3025 1451  103
calculated with TALYS, possibly normalized to experimental data.  3025 1451  104
If the maximal cross section in an excitation function over the   3025 1451  105
whole energy range does not exceed 1.e-9 b, the MT-number is not  3025 1451  106
included. Cross sections lower than 1.e-20 b are assumed to have  3025 1451  107
no physical meaning and are set to zero. All MT numbers extend up 3025 1451  108
to the highest incident energy.                                   3025 1451  109
The following reaction channels/MT numbers are generally included:3025 1451  110
                                                                  3025 1451  111
- MT2      : Elastic scattering cross section: nuclear +          3025 1451  112
             interference terms                                   3025 1451  113
                                                                  3025 1451  114
Obtained by integrating the "nuclear-plus-interference" angular   3025 1451  115
distributions of MF=6.  Note that because of the interference     3025 1451  116
effect, the tabulations in both MF=6 and MF=3 can be negative at  3025 1451  117
some energies and angles.                                         3025 1451  118
                                                                  3025 1451  119
- MT3      : Non-elastic cross section                            3025 1451  120
                                                                  3025 1451  121
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range.    3025 1451  122
                                                                  3025 1451  123
- MT4      : Total inelastic cross section                        3025 1451  124
                                                                  3025 1451  125
Sum of MT=51-91.                                                  3025 1451  126
                                                                  3025 1451  127
- MT5      : (g,anything) cross section                           3025 1451  128
                                                                  3025 1451  129
MT5 contains the reactions which can not be stored in any other   3025 1451  130
MT-number. The information of MF3/MT5 can be combined with        3025 1451  131
MF6/MT5 to obtain residual production cross sections particle     3025 1451  132
production cross sections and (double-)differential cross         3025 1451  133
sections.                                                         3025 1451  134
                                                                  3025 1451  135
- MT16     : (g,2n) cross section                                 3025 1451  136
- MT17     : (g,3n) cross section                                 3025 1451  137
- MT22     : (g,na) cross section                                 3025 1451  138
- MT28     : (g,np) cross section                                 3025 1451  139
- MT51-90  : (g,n') cross section for 1st-40th excited state      3025 1451  140
- MT91     : (g,n') continuum cross section                       3025 1451  141
- MT102    : (g,gamma) cross section                              3025 1451  142
- MT103    : (g,p) cross section                                  3025 1451  143
- MT104    : (g,d) cross section                                  3025 1451  144
- MT105    : (g,t) cross section                                  3025 1451  145
- MT106    : (g,h) cross section                                  3025 1451  146
- MT107    : (g,a) cross section                                  3025 1451  147
- MT600-610: (g,p) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       3025 1451  148
- MT649    : (g,p) continuum cross section                        3025 1451  149
- MT650-655: (g,d) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        3025 1451  150
- MT699    : (g,d) continuum cross section                        3025 1451  151
- MT700-705: (g,t) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        3025 1451  152
- MT749    : (g,t) continuum cross section                        3025 1451  153
- MT750-755: (g,h) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        3025 1451  154
- MT799    : (g,h) continuum cross section                        3025 1451  155
- MT800-810: (g,a) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       3025 1451  156
- MT849    : (g,a) continuum cross section                        3025 1451  157
                                                                  3025 1451  158
##### MF6: PRODUCT ENERGY-ANGLE DISTRIBUTIONS                     3025 1451  159
                                                                  3025 1451  160
In MF6 we store all secondary energy, angle, and energy-angle     3025 1451  161
distributions, as well as all residual and discrete + continuum   3025 1451  162
photon production cross sections for channels other than capture  3025 1451  163
(MT102) and reactions to discrete levels (MT51,etc.).             3025 1451  164
All data are generated with TALYS.                                3025 1451  165
                                                                  3025 1451  166
- MT5      : (g,anything) yields and energy-angle distributions   3025 1451  167
                                                                  3025 1451  168
MT5 contains, in this order,                                      3025 1451  169
For neutrons up to alpha particles and photons:                   3025 1451  170
- the production yields per particle for the whole energy range   3025 1451  171
- the secondary energy-angle distributions per particles which are3025 1451  172
  not represented by explicit MT numbers. On a secondary energy   3025 1451  173
  grid the relative emission spectra are given together with the  3025 1451  174
  parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular              3025 1451  175
  distributions. Inelastic scattering cross sections for discrete 3025 1451  176
  states have been broadened and added to the continuum spectra.  3025 1451  177
For each residual product:                                        3025 1451  178
- the production yields for the whole energy range.               3025 1451  179
- the recoil                                                      3025 1451  180
These distributions can be multiplied with MF3/MT5 to get         3025 1451  181
the production cross sections and (double-)differential cross     3025 1451  182
sections.                                                         3025 1451  183
                                                                  3025 1451  184
- MT16     : (g,2n) energy-angle distr. and photon production     3025 1451  185
                                                                  3025 1451  186
First, for each type of outgoing particle, the (trivial) integer  3025 1451  187
particle yields are given. Next, on a sufficiently dense incident 3025 1451  188
energy grid the secondary energy-angle distributions are          3025 1451  189
specified by means of the relative emission spectra and the       3025 1451  190
parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular distributions. 3025 1451  191
Next, the photon yield is tabulated as a function of incident     3025 1451  192
energy. For each incident energy, the photon production is given  3025 1451  193
for all discrete gamma lines present in the final nucleus. A      3025 1451  194
continuum photon distribution is added to this. We assume         3025 1451  195
isotropy for all produced gamma rays.                             3025 1451  196
                                                                  3025 1451  197
For the following MT-numbers, the same procedure as for MT16 is   3025 1451  198
followed:                                                         3025 1451  199
                                                                  3025 1451  200
- MT17 : (g,3n) energy-angle distr. and photon production         3025 1451  201
- MT22 : (g,na) energy-angle distr. and photon production         3025 1451  202
- MT28 : (g,np) energy-angle distr. and photon production         3025 1451  203
- MT91 : (g,n') continuum energy-angle distr. and phot. prod.     3025 1451  204
- MT649: (g,p) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     3025 1451  205
- MT699: (g,d) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     3025 1451  206
- MT749: (g,t) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     3025 1451  207
- MT809: (g,h) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     3025 1451  208
- MT849: (g,a) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     3025 1451  209
                                                                  3025 1451  210
##### MF8: RADIOACTIVE DECAY DATA                                 3025 1451  211
                                                                  3025 1451  212
For reactions to isomers, MF8 designates where the information    3025 1451  213
for isomeric versus ground state production, or residual          3025 1451  214
production can be found, i.e. for each MT number it points to     3025 1451  215
either MF6, MF9 or MF10.                                          3025 1451  216
                                                                  3025 1451  217
##### MF9: MULTIPLICITIES FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES  3025 1451  218
                                                                  3025 1451  219
MF9 is used for isomer production by non-threshold reactions.     3025 1451  220
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    3025 1451  221
as isomer.                                                        3025 1451  222
                                                                  3025 1451  223
##### MF10: CROSS SECTIONS FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES 3025 1451  224
                                                                  3025 1451  225
MF10 is used for isomer production by threshold reactions.        3025 1451  226
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    3025 1451  227
as isomer.                                                        3025 1451  228
                                                                  3025 1451  229
##### MF33: COVARIANCES OF NEUTRON CROSS SECTIONS                 3025 1451  230
                                                                  3025 1451  231
In MF33, we store the covariances of most, but not all, reaction  3025 1451  232
channels that are given in MF3. All covariances are generated     3025 1451  233
with the TASMAN code [kon19], which generates TALYS runs using    3025 1451  234
a Monte Carlo parameter method.                                   3025 1451  235
For each MT-number, the information consists of:                  3025 1451  236
1. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Ea (variances)                3025 1451  237
2. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Eb (correlations for the      3025 1451  238
   same reaction channel between different energies)              3025 1451  239
3. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Ea (correlations between      3025 1451  240
   different reaction channels for the same energy)               3025 1451  241
4. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Eb (correlations between      3025 1451  242
   different reaction channels for different energies)            3025 1451  243
Classes 1 and 2 are covered by NI-type subsubsections with        3025 1451  244
flag LB=5, followed by a LB=8 section which contains              3025 1451  245
the cross section variances multiplied by 1.e-3. Next, classes 3  3025 1451  246
and 4 are covered by flag LB=6 to account for the different       3025 1451  247
energy grids that two correlating MT-sections may have. Since     3025 1451  248
the total correlation matrix is fully symmetric, we only need to  3025 1451  249
include the correlation elements between different MT-numbers     3025 1451  250
once, and we do that in the subsection with the lowest of the     3025 1451  251
two MT-numbers. We include covariance information for all the     3025 1451  252
total cross sections, the lumped partial cross sections and the   3025 1451  253
individual inelastic cross sections per level (MT51,...). We do   3025 1451  254
not include covariances for MT600 and higher MT-numbers. For the  3025 1451  255
most important channels, we include cross-channel correlations.   3025 1451  256
                                                                  3025 1451  257
##### MF40: COVARIANCES OF ISOMER PRODUCTION                      3025 1451  258
                                                                  3025 1451  259
For covariances of ground state and isomeric cross sections, the  3025 1451  260
same procedures as for MF33 are used.                             3025 1451  261
                                                                  3025 1451  262
